<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krak des Chevaliers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krak des Chevaliers is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> castle in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, on a hill commanding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> Gap between the coast and the interior. The Count of Tripoli granted it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> in 1142, and they rebuilt it into one of the most formidable fortresses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. It fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> in 1271 and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krak-des-chevaliers.jpg" alt="Krak des Chevaliers" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gianfranco Gazzetti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KRAK_DES_CHEVALIERS_-_GAR_-_6-00.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
